WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
Tecmo Bowl, now strap on your helmet and go for it!

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:27.200
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:28.120
I'm Mike.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.240
I'm Sean. I'm Joe.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:31.780
And I'm Sam.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:40.920
I feel like it's the first time in a while where I don't have any qualms with the title of a Nintendo Entertainment System video game.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:42.740
It's very easy.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:46.180
It's very to the point. It's Tecmo's Bowl.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.280
Yeah, it's a bowling game from Tecmo.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:52.540
No. The serial simulator from Tecmo.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.620
It's the Tecmo Bowl, guys.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:59.140
Aren't you excited? Yes.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:00.920
I guess they couldn't say Super Bowl.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:04.580
Well, I guess they will eventually, so that points out of the window.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:12.260
But what about Tecmo Bowl is a football game, and we haven't played a football game since our very first episode, Ten Yard Fight.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:16.220
Sean, you've been waiting for this day, but I'm not going to go to you first.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:17.500
I'm actually going to ask Joe.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:18.780
Joe, have you been anticipating?

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:22.040
Okay, I'm still going to talk first. Joe, have you been anticipating a football game?

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:30.220
Yeah. Ever since Ten Yard Fight really just opened my eyes to how good football games could be, I've been waiting for the next one.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:34.020
Kidding, obviously, because Ten Yard Fight was pretty bare bones.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:35.440
But I knew this was coming.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:39.680
I've heard a lot of good things about it, so I knew it was coming.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.260
And then, Sam, be honest with me.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.340
Is Ten Yard Fight even in your memory anymore?

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:48.120
Can you even think about what we were doing in 2018?

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:50.560
A little bit, yeah.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:56.680
I remember it was vertically oriented, which is kind of weird, I guess.

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:58.180
That's a great memory.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.640
There was a lot of tick-tick-ticking.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:02.320
Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:05.600
Soundtrack. Yeah, that's right.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.300
And it's a port, right?

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.340
Yeah, it was like an arcade game, but so is Tecmo Bowl.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:12.460
So are most of these games.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:15.400
But I'd have to give you a 3 for 3 on your memory.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:17.660
You didn't recall any misinformation.

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:25.480
Yeah. Now, Sean, you always say go Bills at the end of our episodes.

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:27.020
Not always, but most of the time.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:29.100
I'd say you have a 75% trigger rate.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.600
I'd say I've got like a 15% to 20%.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:33.960
Mike cuts them out for all the other ones.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.800
Yeah. We'll let people count them, and they'll get back to us with the actual percentage.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.400
But there are no Bills in this game.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.000
Yeah. Weren't they good during this time?

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:46.820
I mean, they were good.

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:57.140
But this is – so if we're going to get into a little history lesson, this was released the year before the Bills did their historic four Super Bowls in a row run.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:02:59.600
They lost them all.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:01.280
But like this was the year before that.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:11.920
So I imagine that it was based off of the year prior, which this was the bickering Bills era, whereas they were pretty good, but they all kind of hated each other.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:18.420
And it's already a small market, so I'm sure Tecmo wasn't thinking like, that's one of the teams we got to get in there.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:25.760
So I can understand the thought process, but I'm still very personally offended.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:26.900
I can understand.

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:35.240
And you're not the only one from like the Buffalo-Niagara area here, but I don't think Joe is as passionate as you are.

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:39.080
So I'm not even going to ask Joe whether he cares that the Bills are in this game.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:44.280
I'll tell you that I'm a Bills fan because I'm from Buffalo, but I don't follow football too much.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:50.340
So like, you know, when they're playing, I watch, but I'm definitely not as big as Sean on the Bills.

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:57.220
Yeah, I think it's funny because we've had so many episodes recently, Friday the 13th even.

00:03:57.220 --> 00:03:58.980
I know you're going to, you're like, where is he going with this?

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:04.860
But I'm going to be serious. Like there are so many oversights in our knowledge of information.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:07.280
Like we didn't know anything about Friday the 13th.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:08.460
And to be fair, like-

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:09.900
Just like cultural blind spots?

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:11.760
Yeah, but like also like football.

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:13.440
Like you're the only one who really cares.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:18.000
Like this is a huge American like, you know, machine, if you will.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:20.340
Like the, I mean the military caters to the NFL.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:21.520
And here we are.

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:23.240
And we're like, I don't know.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:28.640
I remember, I, you know, I watched some games maybe on TV every now and again.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:30.400
Like we're not, we're not football fans.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:35.900
Thankfully we have Sean though, who will, I'm sure, you know.

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:37.520
I'll fill in the blanks.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:39.220
Yeah, a fair assessment of what we're going with.

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:41.180
But I could have sworn that I had turned you into a football fan.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.740
And it's okay that I haven't, but I thought I did.

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:50.400
You turned me into a football fan as much as like seeing curling on the Olympics turns me into a curling fan.

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:53.940
Like it's on and I watch it and I'm like, this is great and I'm into it.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:58.780
But I'm not checking out the stats during the week or following the storylines, you know.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:05.500
That's okay. Maybe it needs like a, you know, maybe Monday Night Football should become more like Monday Night Raw.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:08.460
You know, maybe there needs to be some scripted moments in these shows.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:10.420
Oh, then you'll love the XFL.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.200
I thought you were about to suggest fantasy curling.

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:15.260
I think you could do it.

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:16.780
I think that's what curling needs.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:21.040
Yeah. Don't be surprised if we never play a curling game on the NES.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:23.120
There's got to be some sort of fantasy curling.

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:25.420
I can't imagine.

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:29.640
Well, there's probably a fantasy league for the Marblelympics, which has curling, right?

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:33.180
I didn't know it has curling, but I bet it has fantasy league for almost anything.

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:36.700
They're actually not allowed to call themselves the Marblelympics anymore.

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:38.040
That's true. Yeah.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:42.740
That stinks. Anyway, enough about other sports and other things and things we don't know about.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:48.680
We do know a little something about NES games and we do know a little bit about how to talk about them.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:56.400
And we're going to prove that right now as our resident football expert, Sean, kind of just gives us a lay of the land of Tecmo Bowl, if you will.

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:01.560
Sean, what is unique about Tecmo Bowl compared to 10-yard fight?

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:03.000
What makes it stand out?

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:04.700
Why are people still talking about this game today?

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:14.420
I think that people still talk about this game today because it actually is the sport of football more or less.

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:23.580
Whereas 10-yard fight was sort of – it was always sort of like an outsider's assumption as to what football could be.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:29.120
I was actually watching some footage of – I didn't play it again because I wasn't feeling it.

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:34.700
But I watched a little bit of footage of 10-yard fight earlier today just because I figured there would be some comparisons.

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:38.940
And it's just like – it's so weird looking.

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:46.500
It looks like another – maybe this would be like how football was played 100 years from now.

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:48.860
But it just doesn't look like it.

00:06:48.860 --> 00:07:01.660
Whereas Tecmo Bowl is – like there are plays to call that sort of make sense and the action is clearer.

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:09.480
There's actual players that were active at the time that are shown in the game.

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:14.280
Whereas they didn't give – they didn't have the rights to the NFL, but they had the rights to the NFL Players Association.

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:21.260
It's just an all-around more accurate and polished version of football.

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:30.100
Yeah, I think we talked in the 10-yard fight episode – maybe I'm misremembering this, but something about like 10-yard fight feeling like –

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:35.680
like oddly reminding us of like one of those wooden peg football games.

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:37.200
You know what I'm talking about? Yes, yes.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:39.580
And like when you really think about that, it doesn't really make sense.

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:41.600
But just play a 10-yard fight.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:48.960
Something about it just feels like – it's just like pieces that are – it feels like a board game to me more than it feels like football.

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:57.340
Where this feels like it's like getting closer to the strategy behind football and there's just like more – just more you can do that –

00:07:57.340 --> 00:08:05.600
it feels like it's a nice bridge between like that primitive era and like what's to come in the future for games like this, I imagine.

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:12.520
Yeah, I think that there's also a lot more control in this game than we got in 10-yard fight.

00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:13.860
As far as I remember.

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:21.740
Oh, yeah. So the – basically I remember in 10-yard fight that if you wanted to just – if you wanted to make call a running play,

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:27.000
you just had to run with the ball and there was a button to pass it to one of your like wingbacks,

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:33.020
which is like a thing that happens in soccer but not football because you were controlling three people at a time.

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:36.420
And – which was also strange.

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:41.960
So it took a lot of weird liberties and didn't really look like football.

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:50.240
And there's a balance here though between like being arcadey and being a sports game.

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:56.620
And I feel like that is something that other games that we've played in the sports genre have kind of either –

00:08:56.620 --> 00:09:04.460
they've either gone simulation or they just – you know, they say who cares about all the rules and everything you need to know and stuff like that.

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:07.480
We're just going to make this a fun game to play and it's super arcadey.

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:10.600
Now, this game has moments like that.

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:14.860
I think about when your players during tackles go like flying in the air.

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:17.040
It kind of reminds me of like an NFL blitz moment.

00:09:17.260 --> 00:09:25.020
Yeah, it's very blitz. There's like – there's silly things like that and the cartoon character style is nice and cute.

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:31.080
But then there's also like you're feeling as if you're playing – you know, I don't know.

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:33.140
I don't play that much John Madden football.

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:36.700
But like this felt pretty close to that at the end of the day.

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:37.980
Yeah, there's only four plays.

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:49.560
But I liked how simple it was and how approachable it was because it seems like everything that you need to do in this game is clearly spelt out for you and is easy for anyone to pick up and play.

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:59.300
And then all of the depth and hidden tricks of the teams and things you can do and, you know, balancing issues.

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:04.660
That's all like for longtime players to kind of figure out as they continue to play the game.

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:09.720
So there's this approachability standpoint that makes it fun to just pick up and play a game.

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:17.500
But then there's also people who are going to want to play through all the weeks of the season and play all the various different teams against the computer.

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:25.920
Yeah. Well, what you said about this sort of feeling – like it's got glimpses of like what Madden will become.

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:37.780
And I think you're right because this game actually gives you that feeling of like the actual rhythm and flow of an American football game.

00:10:37.780 --> 00:10:42.800
Like it's a pretty – like the minutes – I mean the quarters are only a minute and a half long.

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:53.660
But there's enough time that you can sort of feel the narrative forming where like you may have had a slow start but you can make your way back.

00:10:53.900 --> 00:10:57.700
And there are different situations for different plays.

00:10:57.740 --> 00:11:06.960
And it's not just like in a lot of sports games where you're just like spamming shoot or spamming pass and you'll eventually get there.

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:15.420
There's actually – like it's actually a pretty varied experience that it's complex enough to give you.

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:21.860
Real fast though, the clock in this game, it's playing tricks on me all the time.

00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:30.100
It's the shortest amount of time I can imagine being in a football game while also having the slowest clock I've ever seen.

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:37.160
You know, there's something about like you only get two minutes on the clock but it moves much slower than one second I got to imagine.

00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:40.560
Either that or I just am not good at telling what one second feels like.

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:43.160
But it doesn't feel like I'm just playing a two-minute quarter.

00:11:43.780 --> 00:11:50.360
Well, I think it's moving at a second per second but unlike NFL football, the clock is stopping after every play.

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:51.420
Oh, maybe that's it.

00:11:51.580 --> 00:11:57.080
Yeah. It always felt like I had a chance to come back in my games and stuff like that.

00:11:57.120 --> 00:11:58.120
So I appreciate that.

00:11:58.260 --> 00:12:08.080
You know, there's something about these games being short enough to play in a burst but also not – you know, they're not an investment of your time either.

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:12.800
Like you could just play one game against a friend and then be done with it.

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:14.140
You know, like just settle it there.

00:12:14.140 --> 00:12:23.160
Joe, can you tell me about, you know, your thoughts on the game and the gameplay and maybe a specific game that you played?

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:38.700
Yeah. So I have one like – one thing that really jumped out to me specifically about a couple times that I played in which interceptions are really easy.

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:40.400
Did anyone else get that?

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:52.880
Did you get that? I feel like, first of all, I threw a lot of interceptions but I think I caught way more interceptions than I threw to the point where like I felt like it was – it almost made it too easy.

00:12:54.980 --> 00:12:57.600
Did anyone else get that or am I crazy?

00:12:58.060 --> 00:13:00.820
I didn't catch a lot of interceptions but I threw a lot of them.

00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:05.080
I started to get the gist of it after a few games.

00:13:05.080 --> 00:13:13.660
I think – because me and Mike played a couple multiplayer games against each other and I just couldn't for the life of me figure it out.

00:13:13.740 --> 00:13:18.020
I was doing fine against the computer but I just couldn't intercept Mike.

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:26.260
And I think that there are a couple – there's a couple things about interceptions that aren't fully explained.

00:13:26.260 --> 00:13:34.120
Like I think you need to just be better positioned than most people will catch on.

00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:41.980
And maybe a lot of people just subconsciously put themselves in the correct position but for some reason I wasn't.

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:46.600
So I was having a hard time intercepting Mike but I did eventually get the gist of it.

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:51.100
Yeah. Well, that was one thing I was thinking of though because I didn't play against another player.

00:13:51.260 --> 00:13:56.380
I think it would probably be a lot – I think that problem would go away if it wasn't AI.

00:13:56.760 --> 00:14:00.020
You know, that's just from playing the computer.

00:14:02.620 --> 00:14:11.280
And again, I also half the time didn't think I was going to intercept the ball and then suddenly it would just be like interception and like flashing and everything.

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:12.960
And I was like, oh, okay, great.

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:14.260
I guess I intercepted it.

00:14:14.380 --> 00:14:25.460
So I felt like, yeah, maybe I was kind of just – just naturally wherever I positioned myself was always somewhere that the game kind of made it a little easier to intercept.

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:26.800
Wanted you to be, yeah.

00:14:27.260 --> 00:14:32.160
I think it's the frequency of the interceptions though that's like the off-putting part, right?

00:14:32.320 --> 00:14:33.780
It's not that they happen.

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:40.280
It's not like how they happen because honestly, you know, I've seen it happen where you're facing the quarterback.

00:14:40.280 --> 00:14:45.800
So it seems like, yeah, that would be like a fair catch from the defensive team to get that interception.

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:51.340
Then there's ones where like you're like right on top of the guy and you catch it and you're like, okay, I guess.

00:14:51.540 --> 00:14:52.580
Like we're both there.

00:14:52.720 --> 00:14:54.460
I don't understand why he couldn't have caught it instead.

00:14:54.680 --> 00:14:57.300
Maybe it was left to a dice roll, if you will.

00:14:57.400 --> 00:14:58.620
You know, like the game simulates that.

00:14:59.180 --> 00:15:03.620
Or there's other times I think Sean and I ran into one where I was behind him and I caught the ball.

00:15:03.820 --> 00:15:05.120
And I think that was just a glitch.

00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:12.740
I don't know if it's that or if it's just like, because I know that the teams and each individual players has their own stats that are running in the background.

00:15:12.740 --> 00:15:16.620
Like you're saying with the dice rolls, maybe that's part of it too.

00:15:16.740 --> 00:15:24.520
Like it's just a better defensive player that has like a wider range of like interceptionable area.

00:15:24.580 --> 00:15:29.060
I don't know. But the frequency is just higher overall than a regular game.

00:15:29.060 --> 00:15:34.980
And I think stuff like that is okay with me because, again, that's the arcadey experience of it, right?

00:15:35.220 --> 00:15:36.740
You get, yep, it's fast moving.

00:15:36.980 --> 00:15:42.120
There's never like, honestly, if most passes were completion, these would be very high scoring games, right?

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:45.720
You'd be seeing 40 something to 30 something final scores.

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:50.000
Most of the games I played, it was only like 17 to 10 or something like that.

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:56.720
Because of, not just because like the defense is like stopping and there's a lot of punting, but because there are just so many turnovers.

00:15:57.720 --> 00:16:08.360
Right. Yeah, it almost makes me wonder if they were trying to correct the little issues that it being a video game makes it easier to score or something.

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:17.600
And they were trying to kind of like add some RNG that makes it so it broad picture wise feels more like an actual football game.

00:16:17.900 --> 00:16:20.120
Yeah, I think it was like a balanced thing.

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:25.040
Yeah. Even if that ends up changing some of the more specific things that don't feel natural.

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:40.140
Sam, was there anything specific in the games you played that you wanted to call out as like a memorable moment or something that stood out to you from a gameplay point of view?

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:43.840
Just that I was really bad at defense.

00:16:44.120 --> 00:16:53.660
I had to look up like strategies and I can't tell you what they are, but I just could not wrap my head around defense.

00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:57.040
Who would you play as? I played as Team New York.

00:16:57.260 --> 00:16:58.700
Oh, okay. I'm surprised.

00:16:58.700 --> 00:17:02.580
I hear that the New York Giants have a great defense in this game.

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:13.360
I mean, my defense on their own were good, but I was just kind of struggling to the player input side of things was where I struggled.

00:17:13.900 --> 00:17:15.140
Not the... Was it the diving?

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:17.020
Were you diving a lot to nowhere?

00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:26.080
No, I was just like it was hard for me to see who had the ball and who was throwing it because it just happened so quickly.

00:17:26.080 --> 00:17:28.240
And I didn't have that problem on offense.

00:17:28.460 --> 00:17:31.220
Like on offense, I can pick my receiver when I'm throwing it.

00:17:33.140 --> 00:17:36.500
But... And I had no problem running the plays too.

00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:37.740
I think that was fun.

00:17:38.200 --> 00:17:42.100
But there's something about being on defense where I just could not keep track of...

00:17:42.680 --> 00:17:50.200
Like, you know how you press A to switch characters to control which defender you're controlling.

00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:52.200
And that...

00:17:52.860 --> 00:17:57.340
I feel like I never knew when to switch characters.

00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:02.920
And it's just something that I think that I didn't play enough to get better at defense.

00:18:02.920 --> 00:18:08.900
And it would have maybe stuck a little bit better and I would have developed strategies and stuff.

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:14.540
But for my time playing, I really struggled in that regard.

00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:17.800
And so my games were high scoring on both sides.

00:18:18.260 --> 00:18:22.980
I had the exact opposite problem where I was too good.

00:18:23.060 --> 00:18:25.580
No, I'm just kidding. Where I was bad at offense.

00:18:25.860 --> 00:18:27.300
Like I was fine at defense.

00:18:27.300 --> 00:18:28.460
I was intercepting over and over again.

00:18:28.540 --> 00:18:33.420
But like on offense, I couldn't get anything done because of the same reason.

00:18:33.600 --> 00:18:38.600
I could... When you pass, you choose who to pass to by pressing A.

00:18:38.940 --> 00:18:44.840
And I just couldn't split my focus enough between the quarterback and the receivers.

00:18:44.840 --> 00:18:47.720
Yeah, because you're trying to scramble around and not get hit.

00:18:48.060 --> 00:18:49.440
Exactly. And like who's the arrow pointing to?

00:18:49.520 --> 00:18:56.680
Who am I going to pass to? And I end up passing a bunch of interceptions because I wasn't able to look at like who was covered and pass it quickly enough.

00:18:57.080 --> 00:19:02.500
So yeah, it's funny because I had like the exact opposite thing that I was bad at.

00:19:03.400 --> 00:19:16.980
Now, Joe, wouldn't you argue that that experience with the passing, being the quarterback and having to like toggle between the different players, but also keep your eye on defense as they're, you know, at any moment they could sack you.

00:19:17.580 --> 00:19:26.760
Wouldn't you argue that that's like a really true experience to being a quarterback is like how just how quick these guys have to make the call to throw it to the right person?

00:19:26.760 --> 00:19:28.140
I thought about that.

00:19:28.140 --> 00:19:29.180
I thought about that a lot.

00:19:29.300 --> 00:19:30.740
And maybe it loosely translates.

00:19:31.160 --> 00:19:39.540
But when I was really thinking about it, I was like, as when you are a quarterback and you are actually there, you still only have to look in one general direction.

00:19:40.060 --> 00:19:42.680
Or is this I just had to keep glancing back and forth to the quarterback?

00:19:42.940 --> 00:19:44.460
And you know what I mean? Like that's what was good.

00:19:44.660 --> 00:19:46.540
That's what was making me like not be able to see.

00:19:47.660 --> 00:19:49.820
But that's still like across like nine inches.

00:19:49.820 --> 00:19:51.440
I mean, that's true.

00:19:51.580 --> 00:19:59.720
But still, I mean, I guess I guess like I said, I'm saying loosely it probably translates where it's like it's it gives you that level of difficulty.

00:19:59.720 --> 00:20:01.520
But I feel like it's a totally different problem.

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:05.980
I just couldn't see where that arrow was pointing because I wasn't, you know, I wasn't.

00:20:06.280 --> 00:20:13.620
Yeah, I think that there's definitely some timing and reaction to get used to that really it gets you.

00:20:14.220 --> 00:20:21.980
It gets you feeling like very present and in the moment like playing this game because you're like, all right, here's the snap.

00:20:22.140 --> 00:20:24.480
I got to get ready. Got to like make sure I know where my guys are going.

00:20:24.780 --> 00:20:29.740
You know, you're really you really have to sit down and focus when you play, which I think is fun.

00:20:30.140 --> 00:20:32.140
Sean, you don't know how big my screen is.

00:20:33.220 --> 00:20:35.820
I could take this to the movie theater and play it for all you know.

00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:38.520
Maybe I got to really turn my whole head to look between them.

00:20:39.180 --> 00:20:56.020
One thing that I would say about playing defense in football games, I don't think it's ever been solved because I think even in even in modern day Madden, it's still like no one's really having fun playing defense because you can only control one of the 11.

00:20:56.460 --> 00:21:06.600
And so what I would do in my younger years is I would just like always just run at the quarterback and see let the computers take care of everything else.

00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:12.280
And that's sort of what I ended up doing in this game, too, because you can't switch characters.

00:21:12.460 --> 00:21:17.000
You can't switch players in the middle of a play or at least that's what I was.

00:21:17.420 --> 00:21:18.420
That's what I figured.

00:21:18.880 --> 00:21:21.820
There might be some hidden way that you can do it in the middle of a play.

00:21:21.940 --> 00:21:25.600
But as far as I knew, you could you could switch characters before the snap.

00:21:25.820 --> 00:21:37.260
And that was it. So I basically like if I ended up as like a back or like a guy in coverage and I would try and like stick around.

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:41.180
But it's it is a difficult thing to play defense.

00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:51.080
But for some reason, when I was playing against the computer, I barely needed to play defense because the quarterback just kept getting sacked over and over by my own computer.

00:21:52.300 --> 00:21:57.000
Maybe it was because I was playing against Indianapolis and maybe they were just really terrible that year.

00:21:58.220 --> 00:22:01.680
But I kind of that's when I was like, I need to play against somebody.

00:22:01.920 --> 00:22:07.340
And that's why I called in Mike to be to be my multiplayer partner.

00:22:07.960 --> 00:22:14.380
Sean, I want to go back to what you just said, though, about not getting defense right in any football game.

00:22:14.440 --> 00:22:18.400
And I think the problem there and you've described it.

00:22:18.460 --> 00:22:20.320
So I'm just going to break it down into simpler words.

00:22:20.320 --> 00:22:24.980
When you're offense, you are the hero at every time.

00:22:25.200 --> 00:22:30.360
You know, you're the quarterback when you need to pass, when you do pass it and you catch the ball.

00:22:30.640 --> 00:22:39.400
Now you're the receiver and you're going, you know, hopefully for the first down or the touchdown, you know, like you're always the important man when you're on offense.

00:22:39.740 --> 00:22:47.700
When you're on defense, I feel like, you know, and this is probably what Sam was experiencing, too, because he was struggling with the defense.

00:22:47.700 --> 00:22:52.680
You just don't know exactly what to do with yourself.

00:22:52.680 --> 00:22:57.380
You're like, OK, do I do I just become that guy who tries to sack the quarterback every time?

00:22:57.380 --> 00:23:07.560
Should I actually follow the play that I guessed the other team picked and just try to, like, follow that one guy, which is probably the correct answer.

00:23:07.560 --> 00:23:09.540
But not a very exciting thing to do.

00:23:09.700 --> 00:23:19.100
Right. Like, it's not very exciting to just follow somebody who might not even get the ball passed to them or just be, you know, just be one of those guys at the line who.

00:23:19.820 --> 00:23:20.760
What are they called, Sean?

00:23:20.960 --> 00:23:22.920
This is how bad my defensive lineman.

00:23:23.260 --> 00:23:27.080
Yeah. To be a defensive linesman and just, yeah, just do nothing like that.

00:23:27.400 --> 00:23:29.140
It's important and it needs to be done.

00:23:29.340 --> 00:23:32.300
But it seems like the real way to solve this.

00:23:32.540 --> 00:23:38.800
And again, I'm not sure if this would work, but you need to be the hero at every minute during the defense, too.

00:23:38.920 --> 00:23:48.500
You need to be able to, like, switch to switch characters to the, you know, like if you want to be the guy who tries to go for the interception,

00:23:48.500 --> 00:23:50.880
you won't know which one that's getting passed to.

00:23:51.020 --> 00:23:54.060
But if you see it happening, I don't see why you shouldn't be able to.

00:23:54.200 --> 00:23:59.040
And maybe the game could even be smart enough to know that if you pick the switch character button,

00:23:59.300 --> 00:24:02.040
you want to be switched to the one that's most active to the ball.

00:24:02.220 --> 00:24:09.780
And a lot of times in this game, my biggest complaint was just that, like, I'm out of this play and it's up to the computers now.

00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:15.800
You know, like that you're you were running past the 50, past the 40, past the 30.

00:24:15.800 --> 00:24:21.920
I wasn't in that play anymore, but my computer teammates, it was up to them to, like, run up to you and catch you.

00:24:22.620 --> 00:24:29.420
And, you know, maybe that's how it has to be because you're just one man on this team in the grand scheme of things.

00:24:29.700 --> 00:24:30.840
But it felt weird.

00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:33.800
It felt weird to not be in total control of your team.

00:24:33.800 --> 00:24:48.020
And defense, it's a hard thing to translate because in the actual sport, I think that trying to understand defenses is like galaxy brain compared to understanding, like, offense.

00:24:48.140 --> 00:24:50.600
Because you've got different kinds of coverages.

00:24:50.880 --> 00:24:56.000
And what this kind of game, what this game does is it just breaks it down into four plays.

00:24:56.000 --> 00:24:58.300
And I know that that's like, why is it only four plays?

00:24:58.740 --> 00:25:04.620
But I think that that's like all that this game can handle in terms of, like, variations.

00:25:07.080 --> 00:25:10.260
Because it ends up being like a four-way rock, paper, scissors.

00:25:10.260 --> 00:25:16.200
And it sort of balances out the fact that you can only be the one defensive player.

00:25:16.660 --> 00:25:28.720
So that, like, if you were savvy enough to, like, by the situation, pick the play to counter that specific play that the computer ended up running,

00:25:28.720 --> 00:25:37.760
then your computer teammates will be much more responsive and, like, sort of cover your back more.

00:25:39.360 --> 00:25:46.920
Because the other computers players are sort of playing into your hands when you, like, correctly guess that play.

00:25:47.120 --> 00:25:49.560
So I guess there's, like, they tried to balance it that way.

00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:57.840
But I still think that I share your frustration with not being able to switch in the middle of a play.

00:25:57.840 --> 00:26:05.980
And for only having four plays, I think that's actually fine in the scope of what this game set out to be, right?

00:26:06.220 --> 00:26:11.620
Like, you wouldn't want to actually be able to flip through a playbook and decide your move.

00:26:11.760 --> 00:26:13.160
That would, you know, I don't even know how they would do that.

00:26:13.240 --> 00:26:14.920
But, like, I just don't think that's worth it here.

00:26:15.880 --> 00:26:18.840
The problem does come with the rock, paper, scissors thing.

00:26:19.180 --> 00:26:23.040
But I suppose, you know, there's a strategy to that.

00:26:23.040 --> 00:26:31.540
And I think we saw that best in our multiplayer game, Sean, because you sort of started reading me pretty hard as we continued to play.

00:26:32.320 --> 00:26:37.660
And I'd like to think that, you know, at a certain point, you just won't think I'll hit run two anymore.

00:26:38.080 --> 00:26:42.600
But I just kept doing it and you just kept picking it and I just kept getting sacked.

00:26:42.600 --> 00:26:49.780
Well, at a certain point, like, you know, like in a regular football, if you're down 30 points and you like you're not going to run.

00:26:49.940 --> 00:26:55.680
But so the situational game planning sort of translates into this game.

00:26:55.860 --> 00:27:01.720
Like, I think the computer follows a pretty rational game plan as well.

00:27:01.740 --> 00:27:06.320
Like if it finds itself in a third and 13, it's probably going to try and go for a long pass.

00:27:07.600 --> 00:27:16.240
So, like, yeah, if you know the sport, you'll be able to, like, control it correctly situationally, which I also like about it.

00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:18.320
Yeah, I'm glad.

00:27:18.480 --> 00:27:21.060
I think one of you said, you know, it was like a rock, paper, scissors.

00:27:21.360 --> 00:27:23.300
And that's exactly what I was thinking, too.

00:27:23.340 --> 00:27:30.800
And I thought that it worked really well for me just because, you know, if I were to pick up like a Madden game, how do they even do it in a Madden game?

00:27:30.900 --> 00:27:35.120
How do you pick a play in like a more current football game?

00:27:35.120 --> 00:27:39.780
Like, you mean like if you're playing against another person or just in general?

00:27:40.140 --> 00:27:41.880
Just in general. Oh, there's...

00:27:41.880 --> 00:27:44.380
Is it like just a long list of plays or...

00:27:44.380 --> 00:27:52.560
You can group them by formation or by, like, play type or you can hit the pick for me button.

00:27:52.860 --> 00:27:54.000
Ask Madden button.

00:27:54.380 --> 00:27:55.740
Yeah. Yeah, ask Madden.

00:27:56.900 --> 00:28:03.440
But, I mean, yeah, there's an actual, like, I think there's like 60 or something plays per team.

00:28:03.760 --> 00:28:13.660
Yeah. Because, you know, for me, someone who doesn't play football games or really watch a ton of football, it was nice to, like, start out with like, okay, well, it's just like, it's this little rock, paper, scissors type thing.

00:28:14.860 --> 00:28:21.940
I was thinking that, like, that someone like you, Sean, or someone who is more into it would have found that a negative, though.

00:28:22.220 --> 00:28:26.400
I was thinking that, you know, it's like rock, paper, scissors, it's a nice little simple version of the game.

00:28:26.400 --> 00:28:33.440
But, um, I assume people would have wanted a more robust strategy element.

00:28:33.440 --> 00:28:34.400
Or maybe it's just...

00:28:34.980 --> 00:28:36.080
I mean, I would have...

00:28:36.080 --> 00:28:39.140
This game is doing one thing and other games are doing another and they both work, but...

00:28:39.140 --> 00:28:46.780
Yeah, it would have been nice to see, like, oh, I should pick cover two or, like, I should do an all-out blitz or somewhere in the middle somewhere.

00:28:47.620 --> 00:28:48.800
That would have been cool.

00:28:48.800 --> 00:28:53.560
But I think that, like, they've already simplified this game to be an arcade experience.

00:28:54.520 --> 00:29:07.240
I can kind of, like, if it's going to be a defensive play to counter a run, then it's probably going to be, like, the equivalent of, like, a cover one or something.

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:11.380
Like, I know these things don't really make sense to you, but, like, I can kind of...

00:29:11.380 --> 00:29:13.740
I mean, I understand it to an extent.

00:29:13.960 --> 00:29:19.680
I just, just for me, as far as, like, playing a video game, I'm like, I'm not looking to get too deep with it.

00:29:19.760 --> 00:29:23.180
But I just assume people who are more into it would want to.

00:29:23.660 --> 00:29:35.840
Yeah, like, I guess it would make more sense that, like, if I'm trying to counter this pass play, because I think they're going to throw a pass, then I'm, like, I'm basically just going to say, I'll drop my guys into coverage.

00:29:36.080 --> 00:29:44.140
And that's, and that's basically what you're picking by selecting, like, counter this pass play.

00:29:44.340 --> 00:29:47.640
So it's kind of like a roundabout way of doing it.

00:29:47.640 --> 00:29:59.240
I'm sure they could have created the art to show these plays, like, these plays that they weren't just, like, counter this, to counter that, and actually be a defensive play.

00:29:59.480 --> 00:30:01.280
But I don't hold it against it.

00:30:01.540 --> 00:30:11.020
Right, yeah. I mean, I guess there's a difference between, like, being able to just add more plays, but also designing the game properly so it still kind of has that balance that you would get, like, in real life.

00:30:11.020 --> 00:30:15.380
Because I was also thinking, you know, to pick any play, it takes two buttons.

00:30:16.540 --> 00:30:18.500
Yeah. And why not just make it one button?

00:30:18.640 --> 00:30:22.480
Why not just make it one button? You can have six or seven plays instead of just four that way.

00:30:22.700 --> 00:30:31.180
Well, that, I think, is an interesting, not to steamroll this, but I think that the two-button thing is designed for multiplayer.

00:30:31.180 --> 00:30:45.180
Because, like, when you're playing a multiplayer, like, couch multiplayer game, like, with Madden, like, there's four face buttons, and there's usually four plays on the screen at a time.

00:30:45.440 --> 00:30:49.280
So that you're not tipping your hand to your opponent.

00:30:49.560 --> 00:30:51.480
It was just, like, press the face button.

00:30:51.560 --> 00:30:55.080
You don't have to move a cursor to select it and tip off your opponent.

00:30:55.080 --> 00:31:08.480
So I think that since there's only two buttons on the NES controller, that whole two-button combination to make up to four selections is just a way to keep your play call secret.

00:31:09.080 --> 00:31:10.180
Yeah, that makes sense.

00:31:10.320 --> 00:31:11.280
So I appreciated that.

00:31:11.900 --> 00:31:16.920
Just knowing what we know about the defense of the game, too, and our personal complaints with it.

00:31:16.960 --> 00:31:19.200
I'm sure some people love the defense in this game or whatever.

00:31:19.200 --> 00:31:38.280
But it feels like once you open up the ability to have even more plays at your disposal, this rock-paper-scissors mechanic we keep referring to, where if you guess the play of your opponent correctly, that, like, your computer players and you will just be in a better position at the start of the play.

00:31:38.580 --> 00:31:41.040
I think that's kind of what you need.

00:31:42.100 --> 00:31:50.780
You know, that balance of, like, well, if that's the system that they set out to do, the less plays available, the more, like, equal opportunity it becomes.

00:31:50.960 --> 00:32:00.040
You know, if there's 16 plays to choose from, defense only gets worse because now you only have a 1 in 16 chance of picking the correct play.

00:32:00.620 --> 00:32:07.020
Yeah, or it would just be, like, a spectrum of how well you do.

00:32:07.020 --> 00:32:15.960
But, like, even when we were playing coach mode, there were times that I correctly guessed your play, but, like, it was just unlikely for it to be successful.

00:32:15.960 --> 00:32:20.200
But it did, like, kind of be successful a couple times, if you remember.

00:32:20.580 --> 00:32:22.400
So I think it still does that.

00:32:22.540 --> 00:32:24.280
Like, it's just simplified.

00:32:24.280 --> 00:32:26.920
You don't need 16 plays to play this game.

00:32:32.140 --> 00:32:38.160
So let's talk about some of the, you know, arcade-y fun moments of this game.

00:32:38.420 --> 00:32:45.380
You know, we talked about how the NFL Blitz style, like, players falling all over the place and being thrown in the air and stuff like that.

00:32:45.520 --> 00:32:51.400
You can also break out of tackles at any given point, and your dives are pretty ridiculous.

00:32:51.400 --> 00:33:01.440
Were there anything else, maybe presentation or in the gameplay that stood out to you guys as, like, well, you know, this isn't how football's done, but it's fun this way?

00:33:01.660 --> 00:33:05.820
I like that you absolutely smash kicks when you're kicking.

00:33:06.500 --> 00:33:09.660
Like, they just go forever, you know?

00:33:09.820 --> 00:33:14.000
If you fill up the bar all the way, you just send it.

00:33:14.280 --> 00:33:15.320
I love that.

00:33:18.200 --> 00:33:24.740
Yeah, that was another thing, actually, I thought that, you know, we've seen bars that are, like, more difficult even in, like, the golf games and stuff.

00:33:24.840 --> 00:33:28.860
Like, that bar was, I feel like, pretty easy to get, like, at least really close to full.

00:33:29.860 --> 00:33:35.200
Oh, yeah. Yeah, you're actually better off just trying to get, like, close to full than full.

00:33:35.200 --> 00:33:42.200
Because if you go for full, there's a good chance it'll carry over and you'll do the shortest kick of all time in a football game.

00:33:42.200 --> 00:33:51.780
So, you know, the difference between a 100% meter and an 80% meter is probably, like, what, five yards if even?

00:33:51.980 --> 00:33:57.300
So I think it's always better to play it safe than to kick a, you know, a 10-yard punt.

00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:04.560
Yeah. I feel like even sometimes I felt like I was, like, three quarters of the way and it still was, like, I was, like, it was still, like, in the end zone.

00:34:04.560 --> 00:34:19.660
Yeah, I liked that they're, like, in a field goal setting because it's actually pretty easy for the defense to get behind the line and sort of threaten your kicker.

00:34:19.940 --> 00:34:29.680
That if you do get greedy and for some reason want to do a full power kick on an extra point, you're much more likely to get blocked.

00:34:29.680 --> 00:34:32.200
Because you only need, like, half the meter for that.

00:34:32.420 --> 00:34:34.300
And I was blocked many times.

00:34:34.980 --> 00:34:38.860
And there's a ton of depth going on even in the kicking situation.

00:34:39.100 --> 00:34:46.320
Like, which team you pick, the kicker has stats that then determine, you know, the ability that they have.

00:34:46.400 --> 00:34:51.960
And so you could be at the 50-yard line trying to get a field goal.

00:34:52.440 --> 00:34:58.900
And there are teams that I don't care if you get 100%, like, they're not making it, they're not making that field goal.

00:34:59.160 --> 00:35:00.260
Yeah. But other teams can.

00:35:00.720 --> 00:35:08.940
And I think that goes back to just, like, I don't even know how we could talk about this in a podcast episode because we'd have to start, like, actually breaking down characters and stats.

00:35:08.940 --> 00:35:19.140
But it's pretty remarkable that everybody has, like, a speed, a physicality, an offense, you know, a defense.

00:35:19.140 --> 00:35:22.880
Like, how they perform is built into, like, each one of these characters.

00:35:22.880 --> 00:35:26.580
And the game never bothers to let you know that, you know?

00:35:27.640 --> 00:35:35.100
You even find out the names of your players in passing when they do either, like, touchdowns or interceptions and stuff.

00:35:35.240 --> 00:35:37.440
Like, that's the only time you know who's who.

00:35:37.440 --> 00:35:38.860
So it's pretty interesting.

00:35:39.040 --> 00:35:43.520
I'm sure, like, if you play this game enough, you'll know based on the play who's where.

00:35:43.740 --> 00:35:45.300
But that takes extreme knowledge.

00:35:45.560 --> 00:35:48.060
And the game's not exactly giving out that information.

00:35:48.280 --> 00:35:58.340
So there's, like, a lot of, you know, behind the curtains information here that is really enhancing the game for someone like me.

00:35:58.340 --> 00:36:02.100
Because every game can actually be different and go down differently.

00:36:02.760 --> 00:36:04.960
And, yes, the computer's kind of easy.

00:36:05.720 --> 00:36:12.380
But I'm sure, like, if we did try to play, like, a full season and go further down the weeks, I'm sure the computer would pick up, too.

00:36:12.500 --> 00:36:14.700
And you'd get some really interesting situations out of this.

00:36:14.860 --> 00:36:23.720
So I think we just have to appreciate, without being able to go fully in-depth on it, we have to appreciate how much is put into the players and the stats of these guys.

00:36:23.720 --> 00:36:26.240
Because we haven't really seen that in a sports game.

00:36:26.680 --> 00:36:28.620
I know we saw it a bit in baseball.

00:36:29.040 --> 00:36:33.880
But I don't know how, like, I know that they had the stats.

00:36:33.880 --> 00:36:41.000
But were they actually, like, changing the performance of those characters as well?

00:36:41.380 --> 00:36:44.020
In RBI baseball, they definitely were.

00:36:44.300 --> 00:36:50.720
And, obviously, like, in other games that have officially, or any baseball game that you just mentioned, like, yeah, they do have the same.

00:36:50.740 --> 00:36:52.520
I just don't feel, I feel like it doesn't matter as much.

00:36:52.520 --> 00:36:54.600
It doesn't feel as robust, yeah.

00:36:54.840 --> 00:36:56.180
Right, because it's not happening live.

00:36:56.480 --> 00:36:58.920
Baseball is, like, a turn-based game.

00:36:59.060 --> 00:37:01.800
You know, it's your, the pitcher's turn.

00:37:02.180 --> 00:37:03.500
Then it becomes the batter's turn.

00:37:03.740 --> 00:37:05.220
Then it goes to wherever the ball goes.

00:37:05.320 --> 00:37:06.580
Now it's in that fielder's play.

00:37:06.580 --> 00:37:11.340
Here it's, like, your entire team matters all at once and so does the other teams.

00:37:11.420 --> 00:37:20.500
There's a lot of, like, conflicting information happening on that screen that you're directly and indirectly in control of.

00:37:21.480 --> 00:37:29.920
Going back to what you were saying about sort of the goofball moments in the game or, like, the sort of arcadiness,

00:37:30.660 --> 00:37:34.580
I really loved how, I don't think it was on purpose.

00:37:34.580 --> 00:37:43.800
It may have just been a quirk of the code that most of the time there will be a lot of late hits just randomly in the play after the whistle's blown.

00:37:44.240 --> 00:37:46.360
That kind of reminded me of Blitz.

00:37:49.480 --> 00:37:53.280
And the other thing is the ridiculous halftime shows.

00:37:53.340 --> 00:37:55.320
I'm always a fan of a good halftime show.

00:37:55.320 --> 00:38:03.160
So, and I know that it's the same halftime show every time, but those are some great cheerleaders, I gotta say.

00:38:03.280 --> 00:38:05.360
But is it the same halftime show every time?

00:38:05.560 --> 00:38:09.500
Like, don't they change, like, the order of the pictures or something?

00:38:09.760 --> 00:38:11.740
I think they change the order of the pictures, yes.

00:38:11.740 --> 00:38:14.660
But I think it's, like, the same pool of pictures every time.

00:38:14.920 --> 00:38:17.100
I just think that's another nice touch, though, right?

00:38:17.260 --> 00:38:19.720
Like, rather than see the, it's like, oh, which one's gonna come first?

00:38:19.720 --> 00:38:25.540
Like, I bet there are some really rare orders for the halftime show where someone's like, wow, I never saw that one before.

00:38:26.540 --> 00:38:32.000
Well, this one was the throw the cheerleader in the air and then the hot air balloon.

00:38:32.020 --> 00:38:33.260
That was crazy.

00:38:33.840 --> 00:38:49.360
Right. If we're gonna talk about one player, though, it would have to be Bo Jackson, who plays for the Los Angeles Raiders.

00:38:50.260 --> 00:38:53.140
And I don't know, did anybody pick Los Angeles?

00:38:53.300 --> 00:38:54.720
Did anybody play against Los Angeles?

00:38:54.880 --> 00:38:56.380
Were any of us at Bo's mercy?

00:38:56.800 --> 00:38:58.100
No, I, you know what?

00:38:58.100 --> 00:39:03.060
I completely forgot that, because I associate him more with Tecmo Super Bowl.

00:39:04.200 --> 00:39:15.200
Because I remember in Tecmo Super Bowl, it was basically, if you played as Bo Jackson or Thurman Thomas, because they put the bills in that one, your run game was basically unstoppable.

00:39:15.500 --> 00:39:17.300
I think Bo did have a slight edge.

00:39:17.760 --> 00:39:22.400
But no, I actually never played against or with Bo.

00:39:22.540 --> 00:39:25.500
And I feel like it's a, I'm a dummy for not doing that.

00:39:25.500 --> 00:39:35.060
Because no, the number one player in my heart is Kevin Mack, the fullback for the Cleveland Browns.

00:39:35.660 --> 00:39:42.740
That while he was never all that, I mean, he had a couple of Super Bowl, Pro Bowls, but it's not like he was a star player.

00:39:43.600 --> 00:39:50.180
But he basically was my run game for a couple of the games, like one of the games that me and Mike played.

00:39:50.380 --> 00:39:52.540
He's, he will always be number one in my heart now.

00:39:52.540 --> 00:40:03.240
It was something comedic about him, though, because, like, I would, I would run up to him and think like, okay, cool, we're going to get into that, like, tackle fight where you have to spam the A button.

00:40:03.460 --> 00:40:06.540
But no, he would just like, literally, I would just go flying.

00:40:06.820 --> 00:40:16.040
It was like he was, it was like he had, you know, he was like a naked, greasy guy from Family Guy, you know, he just like, you could not get a hold of this guy.

00:40:16.180 --> 00:40:17.060
And I'm like, who is this?

00:40:17.060 --> 00:40:19.000
We actually had to look him up and see his stats.

00:40:19.260 --> 00:40:20.420
I couldn't believe that.

00:40:20.700 --> 00:40:26.500
It seems like none of us were, were interested in taking on Bow, but I did have to see it.

00:40:26.760 --> 00:40:31.680
And I will tell you, it doesn't, like, you know, it was a cursory glance.

00:40:31.720 --> 00:40:34.020
I didn't, I didn't, like, play with him enough or anything like that.

00:40:34.160 --> 00:40:41.760
But I could understand that, like, you know, if you take it to Goldeneye with the no odd job rules and stuff like that.

00:40:41.760 --> 00:40:43.700
But it's probably the same thing here.

00:40:43.820 --> 00:40:47.720
It's just one character that just got way too many stat boosts.

00:40:47.760 --> 00:40:52.400
And it's like, if we can both just agree to not be him, then everybody wins, right?

00:40:52.520 --> 00:40:53.780
Like, there's plenty of other teams.

00:40:53.880 --> 00:40:59.140
It's not like the whole game is the Los Angeles Raiders versus the Miami Dolphins, right?

00:40:59.140 --> 00:41:02.680
Like, if you only had those choices, that that would suck for one player.

00:41:02.900 --> 00:41:09.120
But I think there's enough to go around here that you can kind of avoid, you can kind of avoid Bow.

00:41:09.120 --> 00:41:16.480
That said, there's plenty of guides online on how to deal with him and, like, what to do and stuff like that.

00:41:16.540 --> 00:41:23.760
But from what I understand, Bo Jackson is, like, the Mewtwo of Tech Mobile.

00:41:24.040 --> 00:41:26.000
Finally, some terms I can understand here.

00:41:27.580 --> 00:41:29.460
And he's never played Tech Mobile.

00:41:30.040 --> 00:41:34.880
He said in an interview, to be honest with you, I've seen a kid play that game once.

00:41:34.880 --> 00:41:36.880
I have never played that game.

00:41:36.880 --> 00:41:42.400
I still have the Tech Mobile cassette in a box somewhere in one of my storage places or in the attic.

00:41:42.580 --> 00:41:44.220
But I've never played the game.

00:41:44.360 --> 00:41:52.760
That's crazy, because I feel like if you find out that you're, like, the buffed-up character that's, like, unbeatable in a game, like, how do you not play that game?

00:41:53.560 --> 00:42:05.540
It's funny, because nowadays you'll read about players complaining about their Madden rating or their, like, FIFA rating or whatever, like, in the press.

00:42:05.680 --> 00:42:07.120
So they all play them now.

00:42:07.760 --> 00:42:11.640
If Bo were a modern athlete, he'd be playing as himself all the time.

00:42:12.580 --> 00:42:18.820
Maybe part of the difference is that a lot of people still saw video games as for kids in the 80s.

00:42:18.820 --> 00:42:23.100
Oh, for sure. Rather than now, where it's, like, everyone's, you know, 20s and 30s that play video games.

00:42:24.140 --> 00:42:29.060
And also, this happened before, too, with Mike Tyson never played Mike Tyson's Punch-Out.

00:42:29.460 --> 00:42:33.020
And I think he was actually, like, not a fan of the game or what have you.

00:42:33.140 --> 00:42:37.280
But then it was either Jimmy Fallon or Jimmy Kimmel had him play it.

00:42:37.280 --> 00:42:38.820
One of those. Well, you know what?

00:42:38.820 --> 00:42:45.640
They're both awful. On one of their Tonight Shows, they had him play the game.

00:42:45.720 --> 00:42:55.580
And it was, like, they just should have – somebody should have, like, play-tested that idea first of what it would be like if you never played a video game, for all we know.

00:42:55.580 --> 00:43:01.800
And then they tell you to, like, go up against Mike Tyson from Mike Tyson's Punch-Out against real-life Mike Tyson.

00:43:02.120 --> 00:43:05.240
It was just – I mean, like, maybe I'll put it in the show notes.

00:43:05.240 --> 00:43:07.380
Maybe I won't because I don't want to give these guys any more views.

00:43:07.700 --> 00:43:18.520
But there's something about how awful he was that I wouldn't want to see Bo Jackson play Tecmo Bowl because it would just be disappointing to the in-game character.

00:43:18.740 --> 00:43:19.880
Why do you hate Jimmy Fallon?

00:43:21.340 --> 00:43:22.600
He has never been funny.

00:43:22.600 --> 00:43:25.040
He just slams his desk and laughs as hard as he can.

00:43:25.040 --> 00:43:28.020
He's not funny. He's just such a friendly guy.

00:43:28.680 --> 00:43:29.500
Yeah, he's a friendly guy.

00:43:29.500 --> 00:43:31.140
I don't mean that he did anything wrong.

00:43:31.260 --> 00:43:33.500
I want to make it clear. I'm not canceling him.

00:43:38.660 --> 00:43:47.000
So there's one player, two player, and then there's coach mode, which is also just for two players.

00:43:47.200 --> 00:43:52.560
There's no coach mode with you versus the comm, which I thought was just a weird choice.

00:43:52.760 --> 00:43:54.200
Why couldn't that be done?

00:43:54.200 --> 00:43:55.780
I will never find out.

00:43:56.000 --> 00:43:58.340
But you do need two players to play coach mode.

00:43:59.240 --> 00:44:07.380
And essentially, it's the same exact game, except for now you're only ever just guessing what the other opponent is picking as their play.

00:44:08.180 --> 00:44:09.560
And I don't think we ever made that clear.

00:44:09.720 --> 00:44:12.580
And I don't think we ever made that clear, actually. Can I actually just make that clear for the audience?

00:44:13.300 --> 00:44:14.940
Sure. Most people don't pick plays.

00:44:15.460 --> 00:44:25.520
Only the offensive picks a play, and defense sees the offense's plays and then gets to choose which one they think the offense is going to pick.

00:44:25.640 --> 00:44:27.480
There is no defensive playbook.

00:44:27.480 --> 00:44:29.540
Yeah, that's sort of what I was trying to get at earlier.

00:44:29.540 --> 00:44:34.980
But yeah, it's just showing you these are the things that this team does.

00:44:35.180 --> 00:44:37.760
Like whatever team on offense, these are their plays.

00:44:38.000 --> 00:44:43.120
And your defense just sort of will cater to stopping those plays.

00:44:43.120 --> 00:44:50.800
So with that said, you know, because each team has their own playbook and, you know, even their own amount of possible plays, too.

00:44:50.960 --> 00:44:57.880
Like some people will have only one running play and three passing plays, or they'll have an equal amount of running and passing plays.

00:44:59.160 --> 00:45:01.320
And those running and passing plays are different.

00:45:01.320 --> 00:45:13.760
Even if they're titled running play one or passing play one, it's a different play per team, which is great, too, because that really helps make the teams feel different other than just the stats.

00:45:14.080 --> 00:45:17.680
Again, another thing that you couldn't necessarily do in baseball.

00:45:17.840 --> 00:45:22.160
There's no like, oh, well, this team's, you know, catcher signs are just like unbeatable.

00:45:22.420 --> 00:45:23.840
You know, like that just doesn't happen.

00:45:24.360 --> 00:45:26.220
So, Sean, coach mode, what's the verdict?

00:45:28.100 --> 00:45:34.940
I think as basically just because there's been a few games that we played that have like a spectate mode.

00:45:35.620 --> 00:45:39.260
Yeah. Which I find to be weird.

00:45:39.540 --> 00:45:42.360
But I guess if it's possible, then add it.

00:45:42.620 --> 00:45:50.820
I think this is a step up from a spectate mode because like you still like you basically are just the coach.

00:45:51.440 --> 00:46:01.720
And you decide whether you're going to punt on fourth down or if you're going to try and go for it, if you're going to try to go for a field goal.

00:46:02.540 --> 00:46:04.220
You're going to throw or you're going to pass.

00:46:05.180 --> 00:46:08.560
But you don't get to directly influence any of that.

00:46:08.960 --> 00:46:11.600
And like people want to do that.

00:46:11.720 --> 00:46:19.320
Like even in 2009 or something, EA came out with a NFL head coach game where you didn't play the game.

00:46:19.320 --> 00:46:23.800
But it was basically just watching Madden and playing and picking the plays.

00:46:24.020 --> 00:46:26.080
So there's always been a market for this.

00:46:26.080 --> 00:46:31.240
I think it's a really neat add-on to the game.

00:46:31.940 --> 00:46:33.860
And I guess I'm pretty good at it.

00:46:35.640 --> 00:46:38.440
Yeah, you need to do a little more playtesting than me.

00:46:38.800 --> 00:46:40.440
But I thought you were great.

00:46:40.540 --> 00:46:45.240
And I think that there is something different going on in that mode than just playing the game.

00:46:45.620 --> 00:46:49.180
It feels like something you could talk your like father into.

00:46:49.560 --> 00:46:52.380
You know, you could be like, hey, dad, like play video games with me.

00:46:52.480 --> 00:46:53.800
You can just pick the plays.

00:46:53.800 --> 00:46:55.300
You don't have to actually do anything.

00:46:55.600 --> 00:46:58.120
I feel like most dads would be down for that.

00:46:58.300 --> 00:47:03.360
Sure. There is something though about like why not just also include a watch mode.

00:47:03.540 --> 00:47:08.600
I don't think anybody would have a gripe if that was just in there to watch virtual football.

00:47:08.760 --> 00:47:11.680
There's something like novel about that in 1989.

00:47:12.300 --> 00:47:14.440
You know, football is only on on Sundays.

00:47:14.440 --> 00:47:18.700
Now it's like I could just watch football any day of the week on my simulated Tecmo Bowl.

00:47:19.460 --> 00:47:22.200
You're right. They definitely could have.

00:47:22.200 --> 00:47:26.100
I mean, all of the the all the AI is there for it.

00:47:26.200 --> 00:47:33.400
I guess. I guess I just didn't think that they thought that coach mode was enough for you people.

00:47:34.260 --> 00:47:40.480
Well, see, my thing with coach mode, which brings me back to I think coach mode is really cool.

00:47:40.640 --> 00:47:42.240
Actually, I think it's a cool idea.

00:47:42.600 --> 00:47:50.280
I do wish, though, then with coach mode, you'd have like more robust strategies available to you or just more options.

00:47:50.880 --> 00:47:55.100
Because now it feels like coach mode just does sort of become like playing.

00:47:55.100 --> 00:47:58.920
This is that's also where the rock, paper, scissors really shows itself.

00:47:59.120 --> 00:48:08.300
Like because like your performance in any given play will just vary because you're a human and you do things differently when you repeat them.

00:48:08.300 --> 00:48:17.900
But when you play the coach mode, it's pretty obvious that like if you guess that play correctly, it's either going to be like a sack or an interception.

00:48:19.200 --> 00:48:21.080
It's very binary that way.

00:48:21.240 --> 00:48:29.980
Right. And Joe, to go to your point, though, about like in coach mode should have had something in terms of strategy or or the like.

00:48:30.180 --> 00:48:33.860
There isn't much else here other than the four plays.

00:48:34.040 --> 00:48:44.600
And then if you're on the fourth down, you get the option to go for the field goal or do the or do the punt kick or just go for it.

00:48:44.660 --> 00:48:47.980
Right. But there's there's you can't fake a kick.

00:48:47.980 --> 00:48:55.800
You can't you know, there's no like special teams in the in the way that trick plays could be done or something like that.

00:48:56.020 --> 00:48:58.000
And I'm not saying that that needed to happen here.

00:48:58.120 --> 00:49:05.220
But we also just need to address like the lack of actual rules that this game is recognizing compared to real football.

00:49:05.420 --> 00:49:13.820
We've had a great time talking about how it feels like football, but somehow they've managed to avoid a lot of what real football actually is.

00:49:13.860 --> 00:49:16.020
And we we've let them get away with it up until now.

00:49:16.020 --> 00:49:24.320
So the fact that there's like no penalties, there's no you can't run out the lines other than during passing plays.

00:49:25.400 --> 00:49:31.180
I'm trying to get some other things, you know, that the fake punt seemed like one that like that's that could be an easy thing to select.

00:49:31.360 --> 00:49:32.520
But how do you select that?

00:49:32.680 --> 00:49:33.560
You know, I understand.

00:49:33.800 --> 00:49:34.800
What do you guys think?

00:49:34.960 --> 00:49:41.140
Did we did we not give them a fair shake here on the on the things that they dropped the ball on?

00:49:41.140 --> 00:49:51.940
I feel like due to the arcadey nature of this, like adding rules and adding anything that would stop the flow of the game would be a detriment to it.

00:49:52.160 --> 00:49:57.460
And so I think that it's I think the choices they've made are more than fair.

00:49:57.460 --> 00:50:08.020
And I think it's also just a frustration removal thing, because the two most common penalties in football are false starts in the holding.

00:50:08.020 --> 00:50:25.240
And when you are with whatever position you're controlling in this game, it won't be because of you, because like when you're on offense, you're not going to be playing as a lineman who holds a guy so they can't get to the quarterback.

00:50:25.920 --> 00:50:29.240
So it's going to be a random thing that a computer does.

00:50:29.360 --> 00:50:38.060
And that would make me pull my hair out if in this game I just was getting random false starts because my team has a low discipline rating or something.

00:50:39.340 --> 00:50:41.820
I don't think that this kind of game needs that.

00:50:42.020 --> 00:50:47.060
I pull my hair out when I'm playing Madden, like modern Madden, and I get a false start because that wasn't me.

00:50:47.200 --> 00:50:53.020
That was just the computer rolling a die and saying this is going to be a penalty play.

00:50:53.020 --> 00:50:58.440
So I think it's OK that they don't really have penalties in this.

00:50:58.600 --> 00:51:04.660
And they also don't really have incompletions in this unless you weirdly throw the ball out of bounds randomly.

00:51:06.360 --> 00:51:07.200
Completely agree.

00:51:07.420 --> 00:51:09.620
Completely agree here. Just pose the question out there.

00:51:09.740 --> 00:51:21.080
But really, like for me, I'm the guy who when I was a kid and I had Super Mario 64, I would talk to the same NPCs over and over again because I thought there was a chance they'd say something different.

00:51:21.080 --> 00:51:25.220
You know, like that's how that's how gullible I was as a kid in video games.

00:51:25.380 --> 00:51:35.460
And then I remember it was some version of Madden, maybe like Madden 08 or 07 or something where they introduced challenges.

00:51:35.460 --> 00:51:38.380
Like you could actually challenge the play on the field.

00:51:38.380 --> 00:51:43.760
And my friend had the game and I remember him like just showing it to me and I saw that challenges was an option.

00:51:43.900 --> 00:51:46.280
And I was like, how would that be an option?

00:51:46.400 --> 00:51:48.560
Like why would it's a it's a video game.

00:51:48.780 --> 00:51:50.260
Why would it make a mistake?

00:51:50.440 --> 00:51:52.780
The ref should just know everything.

00:51:53.180 --> 00:51:55.980
You should never have to challenge because it's a video game.

00:51:55.980 --> 00:52:04.480
So I am like I'm on both ends of this spectrum where like if the game lends itself to the style, I'm in that world.

00:52:04.480 --> 00:52:16.380
But when you're trying to simulate like, you know, an actual sports game and then you allow for error and especially computer error on your own team, I don't think I don't think it works.

00:52:16.380 --> 00:52:35.560
Yeah, I think that it definitely lends itself of that authenticity that I would bring up in most of these sports game talks to have that weird like gray area of the computer knows that this is a bad call.

00:52:36.480 --> 00:52:44.320
But it's just giving you so that it's like allowing that feature of challenges to express itself.

00:52:44.900 --> 00:52:47.160
But I still I agree with you.

00:52:47.280 --> 00:52:49.860
It is weird that that's even in there at all.

00:52:50.920 --> 00:52:52.840
And it definitely wouldn't work here.

00:53:25.000 --> 00:53:33.440
So we had some roster changes through tech mobile and I'm I'm not sure you know, sometimes it depends on which version of the game you get a hold of.

00:53:33.440 --> 00:53:41.100
But I won't get into the players names or anything like that because it's not important because we never really talked about the stats anyway.

00:53:41.380 --> 00:53:44.380
But there's the original tech mobile for the NES.

00:53:44.660 --> 00:53:50.740
Okay, and then there's a later version that was released with a white and gold seal on it.

00:53:50.740 --> 00:53:57.060
And that has like, I think, either one or two different players compared to the original tech mobile.

00:53:57.300 --> 00:54:00.860
I'm not sure like why the decision was made to change those guys.

00:54:01.060 --> 00:54:16.120
But then a year later, the Japanese Famicom version of tech mobile came out and it has like a bunch of different roster changes because there's like it was released technically in 1990.

00:54:16.120 --> 00:54:26.740
So I think they tried to change the teams or the players to be based off of those new stats from the 1989 year instead of the 1988 year.

00:54:26.920 --> 00:54:37.060
I just think it's amazing that they went through the effort of technically changing the roster on what are essentially like revision changes of tech mobile, not actual new games.

00:54:37.240 --> 00:54:39.520
And that's sort of carried on to Madden.

00:54:41.040 --> 00:54:45.220
Yeah, and FIFA and all those other games now, but they released them as new titles.

00:54:45.540 --> 00:54:49.720
This was only ever called tech mobile, tech mobile, tech mobile.

00:54:49.960 --> 00:54:54.840
But there's three different versions of this game that I'm not sure if collectors even collect the differences.

00:54:55.180 --> 00:54:56.320
That's interesting. Huh.

00:54:56.680 --> 00:54:57.880
I mean, I'm sure somebody does.

00:54:58.020 --> 00:54:59.560
I'm sure the people who now know about it.

00:54:59.620 --> 00:55:05.620
I'm sure I just made the price of complete in box original copies of tech mobile without the white and gold seal.

00:55:05.740 --> 00:55:06.620
That just skyrocketed.

00:55:06.620 --> 00:55:10.840
That's what we do here. We manipulate the collector's market.

00:55:11.140 --> 00:55:16.340
We may or may not have bought 1,000 copies of the original tech mobile before this episode came out.

00:55:17.720 --> 00:55:19.260
And sequels and spinoffs.

00:55:19.400 --> 00:55:21.320
You guys know you want them, right?

00:55:21.500 --> 00:55:23.980
Who doesn't want sequels and spinoffs to football games?

00:55:24.240 --> 00:55:30.300
That's me. Imagine if I just start listing off like Madden, Madden 64, you know, like just keep going.

00:55:30.460 --> 00:55:32.440
No, we're only talking about the tech mobile games here.

00:55:32.440 --> 00:55:39.140
So, obviously, the arcade version came first and we didn't really mention that at all throughout this because there are kind of two different games.

00:55:39.400 --> 00:55:42.460
The original arcade version is gigantic.

00:55:42.660 --> 00:55:48.680
It's got two huge monitors and it is an upright arcade cabinet.

00:55:48.880 --> 00:55:52.880
So, it's not like – it takes up a lot of space I guess is what I'm trying to say.

00:55:53.000 --> 00:55:54.920
But it also supports four players.

00:55:55.720 --> 00:55:57.640
So, I thought, oh, that's kind of interesting.

00:55:57.640 --> 00:56:01.780
You could do 2v2, never 3v1.

00:56:01.960 --> 00:56:06.220
Should be four teams, four separate teams all playing at once on a big square.

00:56:06.460 --> 00:56:07.660
Right, and it's a circle.

00:56:07.800 --> 00:56:08.620
No, it's a circle.

00:56:09.600 --> 00:56:16.460
Also, the arcade version only has two fictional teams, the Wildcats and the Bulldogs.

00:56:16.600 --> 00:56:17.840
That's all you can play as.

00:56:18.220 --> 00:56:24.660
No player stats or – well, obviously, player stats built into those teams, but no NFLPA.

00:56:25.100 --> 00:56:26.300
I guess that's all you need.

00:56:26.300 --> 00:56:27.320
Why not, right?

00:56:27.420 --> 00:56:29.120
Especially if it's just putting in quarters.

00:56:29.420 --> 00:56:33.860
You really have to select – like, oh, if I can't play as the New York Giants, I'm going home.

00:56:35.300 --> 00:56:39.920
Tecmo Super Bowl is our next – we will play that one.

00:56:40.440 --> 00:56:42.820
It's going to come up sometime for the NES.

00:56:43.000 --> 00:56:44.840
Not too soon, but it will happen.

00:56:44.960 --> 00:56:47.360
And this one should have the bills.

00:56:47.900 --> 00:56:54.100
It will. It's automatically a better game by everybody on this podcast's opinion.

00:56:54.100 --> 00:56:57.820
Tecmo Super Bowl II Special Edition.

00:56:58.200 --> 00:57:01.080
Why does this sound like a Street Fighter game?

00:57:02.720 --> 00:57:06.200
That's because all the different, like, additions to the title.

00:57:07.060 --> 00:57:11.060
Yeah, and the Roman numeral II and Super in it.

00:57:11.200 --> 00:57:12.220
Like, this is crazy.

00:57:13.260 --> 00:57:17.180
Yeah, and Tecmo Super Bowl II Special Edition and Knuckles.

00:57:17.180 --> 00:57:18.480
Knuckles. Remastered.

00:57:18.580 --> 00:57:25.940
So if you plug this into the Sonic and Knuckles, like, double-decker thing, it would – there would be another team of just Knuckleses?

00:57:26.260 --> 00:57:27.460
I think that's how that works.

00:57:27.600 --> 00:57:28.660
Yeah. Yep.

00:57:29.920 --> 00:57:32.520
That's funny, but thankfully that doesn't happen.

00:57:32.720 --> 00:57:40.520
Instead, Tecmo Super Bowl II seems to make a lot of interesting changes that Joe seemed to have been requesting this entire episode.

00:57:40.740 --> 00:57:44.240
So in Tecmo Super Bowl II, you can now trade players.

00:57:44.680 --> 00:57:46.800
There's a 37-team roster.

00:57:47.620 --> 00:57:49.820
Defensive players can now be injured.

00:57:50.420 --> 00:57:58.280
The fair catch and two-point conversion, which were first introduced in this same year that the game was released, are now added into the game.

00:57:58.480 --> 00:58:02.600
And the offensive playbook was expanded to 16 plays.

00:58:02.960 --> 00:58:04.120
I'm glad I was heard.

00:58:04.320 --> 00:58:07.380
I'm glad that they heard me and they followed through.

00:58:08.220 --> 00:58:09.880
Do you think that that had to be pitched?

00:58:10.060 --> 00:58:13.320
Like, somebody had to go to an exec and be like, I got it.

00:58:13.720 --> 00:58:14.680
16 plays.

00:58:14.780 --> 00:58:19.060
To be honest, I think – you got me thinking about this a minute ago when you were talking about the arcade version.

00:58:19.060 --> 00:58:22.780
I think that maybe this was like, okay, well, we have this game already for the arcade.

00:58:22.920 --> 00:58:23.820
Let's port it to the NES.

00:58:24.120 --> 00:58:28.560
And then they were, like, rethinking, like, what's the best way to have something that's in someone's home?

00:58:29.020 --> 00:58:34.680
And then they maybe started thinking about making it a little more robust in that way.

00:58:35.100 --> 00:58:42.460
I mean, they probably already made that change by adding – like you guys said, adding the teams and everything, which, like, wouldn't be as necessary in the arcade.

00:58:42.640 --> 00:58:43.800
So that's my guess.

00:58:43.800 --> 00:58:47.460
And I also – I'm just kind of guessing here as well.

00:58:47.480 --> 00:59:06.760
But I would say that as people get more familiar with menu diving and just kind of using – or just video game interfaces and, you know, something like a 16-option playbook may have been, like, a lot to handle at this time.

00:59:09.200 --> 00:59:16.920
Whereas in the future, in the early 90s, people kind of can wrap their heads around it a little bit more.

00:59:17.180 --> 00:59:18.640
Yeah, you're hungry for it now.

00:59:18.720 --> 00:59:20.320
You've had the taste of four.

00:59:20.500 --> 00:59:21.840
You're like, oh, I hope they do eight.

00:59:21.960 --> 00:59:24.260
And once they do eight, you're like, they better be doing 16.

00:59:26.520 --> 00:59:30.740
I don't know why Madden every year doesn't just, you know –

00:59:30.740 --> 00:59:33.260
Double it? Yeah, double the number, right?

00:59:33.340 --> 00:59:36.140
There should be 1,048 plays by now.

00:59:36.740 --> 00:59:37.900
Gets more than that.

00:59:38.560 --> 00:59:40.320
Yeah. You know what I mean.

00:59:40.620 --> 00:59:42.380
I was just saying, like –

00:59:42.380 --> 00:59:43.840
Exponentially more plays.

00:59:43.860 --> 00:59:47.800
You know what I was saying. Right, right.

00:59:48.160 --> 00:59:50.140
There should be 500,000.

00:59:50.140 --> 00:59:55.040
There should be as many residents in Massapequa as there are plays in a playbook.

00:59:55.060 --> 00:59:55.960
Before we started recording.

00:59:58.880 --> 01:00:05.480
Okay. After Tecmo Super Bowl II Special Edition comes Tecmo Super Bowl III Final Edition.

01:00:06.280 --> 01:00:07.620
That's a weird thing to say.

01:00:08.020 --> 01:00:10.040
Seems pretty declarative, too, though.

01:00:10.160 --> 01:00:14.660
Like, the final edition of Tecmo Super Bowl or of Tecmo Bowl in general?

01:00:14.660 --> 01:00:19.540
What do you think? I mean, it sounds – because I'm looking at Wikipedia right now.

01:00:19.540 --> 01:00:23.440
It seems like for a while, like, they held their end of the deal with that.

01:00:24.380 --> 01:00:26.140
Like, that was the last Tecmo Bowl game.

01:00:26.780 --> 01:00:29.280
And most people will tell you they should have just stuck to that, too.

01:00:29.440 --> 01:00:34.160
That that was their decision and they should have just, you know, sat it out after Final Edition.

01:00:34.180 --> 01:00:37.260
I think that's why you could always – you only get one final.

01:00:38.160 --> 01:00:40.560
Right? Yeah. No, Final Fantasy.

01:00:40.820 --> 01:00:42.640
We're not talking about them. Oh.

01:00:43.280 --> 01:00:45.860
Yeah, yeah, yeah. Well, they're just still working on it, though.

01:00:45.940 --> 01:00:47.380
It's still their Final Fantasy.

01:00:47.800 --> 01:00:50.240
They're just – yeah. They're starting Chapter 16 now.

01:00:50.480 --> 01:00:56.120
It just does seem like such a weird decision to – like, who would make that decision to call this final?

01:00:56.220 --> 01:00:57.280
Like, what if – I don't know.

01:00:57.360 --> 01:00:58.460
It just seems like a weird business decision.

01:01:00.340 --> 01:01:03.300
Yeah, like, they saw how much money they were making from the series and they were like,

01:01:03.440 --> 01:01:05.580
okay, we probably have enough to make just one more.

01:01:05.580 --> 01:01:11.240
Right. Like, we don't want to make any more after this because the whole marketing gimmick of this one is it's the last one.

01:01:11.860 --> 01:01:18.320
Anyway, Tecmo Super Bowl III Final Edition lets you do a couple of additional things that Tecmo Super Bowl II didn't let you do.

01:01:18.860 --> 01:01:24.960
Now, you can switch out plays and put another in its place in the middle of the game.

01:01:25.120 --> 01:01:26.140
Oh, they got audibles in there. Nice.

01:01:26.560 --> 01:01:28.400
Yeah. In the middle of the game, you can do that.

01:01:28.400 --> 01:01:37.180
And there's a superstar editor which allows for you to create your own player and you use a limited point system and then you figure it out.

01:01:37.280 --> 01:01:39.020
It's basically modern-day creative player.

01:01:39.180 --> 01:01:40.580
And now you can play as that player.

01:01:40.700 --> 01:01:44.540
So, Sean, you would obviously be the quarterback for the Bills in this game.

01:01:44.920 --> 01:01:46.200
I would just make Josh Allen.

01:01:46.880 --> 01:01:48.140
Nice. Instead of yourself.

01:01:48.260 --> 01:01:57.140
Very good. There is a free agency in the game, too, that you have, like, three weeks or something beforehand to sign or something like that.

01:01:57.240 --> 01:01:58.340
Or, no, that's how the trades work.

01:01:58.500 --> 01:01:59.640
Free agency is a little different.

01:01:59.640 --> 01:02:00.580
But it's in the game.

01:02:00.900 --> 01:02:05.160
And then depending on the home team, now there's different stadium types, too.

01:02:06.040 --> 01:02:07.000
Oh, I dig it.

01:02:07.340 --> 01:02:08.380
I like that.

01:02:08.460 --> 01:02:14.160
There's, like, actual, like, closed roof stadiums and open stadiums and stuff like that.

01:02:14.160 --> 01:02:15.020
Yes. Yeah. Yep.

01:02:15.160 --> 01:02:27.680
Absolutely. So, Tecmo Super Bowl III seems just like we just keep improving, which honestly makes me, because I left out, obviously, for important reasons, all the information on Tecmo Super Bowl I, because we're actually going to play that one.

01:02:27.820 --> 01:02:35.900
It makes me wonder, how will they improve the formula from Tecmo Bowl to Tecmo Super Bowl when we're already seeing so many changes in Super Bowl II and Super Bowl III?

01:02:36.140 --> 01:02:37.660
I still have hopes.

01:02:39.060 --> 01:02:44.800
All right. We're running a little fat here, so I'm just going to say that they made a DS game called Tecmo Bowl Kickoff.

01:02:45.540 --> 01:02:51.800
And I can't speak to how true it stays to the Tecmo franchise, but I will say that it adds superpowers.

01:02:52.520 --> 01:02:56.840
There you go. It's pretty, truly not that true to the series.

01:02:58.220 --> 01:03:04.240
A Wii version was also announced, but it was canceled and retooled into a different game.

01:03:04.320 --> 01:03:06.600
And I didn't know you could cancel Wii games.

01:03:06.760 --> 01:03:09.340
There is so much shovelware on that system.

01:03:09.800 --> 01:03:11.300
Thousands of games on the Wii.

01:03:11.520 --> 01:03:12.820
And they canceled one?

01:03:12.940 --> 01:03:14.680
Yeah, I feel like you just release whatever you have.

01:03:14.860 --> 01:03:15.920
Just release it. Right.

01:03:16.380 --> 01:03:17.900
It'll hold up. I think that's the way to go.

01:03:17.960 --> 01:03:20.080
Tecmo Super Bowl Unfinished Edition.

01:03:20.080 --> 01:03:22.420
Yeah, they made eight Cooking Mamas.

01:03:22.600 --> 01:03:28.580
Might as well. Definitive Edition. It's just like a Bible.

01:03:28.840 --> 01:03:30.340
It's just like a pitch deck.

01:03:30.540 --> 01:03:33.040
And you just go through a menu using the Wiimote.

01:03:33.120 --> 01:03:33.920
And you're like, oh, yeah.

01:03:35.500 --> 01:03:41.060
And last, Tecmo Bowl throwback for the 360 and the PS3.

01:03:41.500 --> 01:03:49.720
It's that same era of time where people just use shitty 3D graphics and low budgets to revive classic games.

01:03:49.720 --> 01:03:51.920
But, you know, the graphics weren't ready yet.

01:03:52.060 --> 01:03:58.560
And also, nobody wanted to see these games get remade into 3D instead of just being Tecmo Bowl again.

01:03:59.440 --> 01:04:01.840
So, I'm not even going to give this one a chance.

01:04:01.940 --> 01:04:03.200
I'm just letting you know it exists.

01:04:03.520 --> 01:04:07.140
And it's only available on Xbox 360 Arcade and PS3 Downloads.

01:04:07.220 --> 01:04:08.360
So, good luck playing it.

01:04:08.840 --> 01:04:12.580
Jeez. But we've had a long time to play Tecmo Bowl.

01:04:12.700 --> 01:04:14.680
We've had a long time to talk about Tecmo Bowl.

01:04:14.680 --> 01:04:21.560
And now, it is finally time to decide if Tecmo Bowl is going to be on the Essential Games List.

01:04:24.160 --> 01:04:30.020
Joe, I'd like to start with you for your vote on Tecmo Bowl.

01:04:30.620 --> 01:04:31.800
These are always tough for me.

01:04:32.080 --> 01:04:37.860
As I think I say every time we have a sports game or a shmup or just a game that's not something I would usually seek out.

01:04:38.800 --> 01:04:44.760
Because I did think that this is one of, if not maybe the best sports game I think we've played so far.

01:04:45.180 --> 01:04:48.960
But we always talk about that sort of barrier to entry that sports games have.

01:04:48.960 --> 01:04:53.320
And I kind of was playing around with the idea on this one.

01:04:53.440 --> 01:04:57.100
But the idea of kind of skipping that this time.

01:04:57.280 --> 01:05:10.240
But I do think that based on, first of all, partially based on just learning now that it seems like the sequel will kind of add some things I was looking for.

01:05:11.260 --> 01:05:20.200
And just based on the fact that as much as I think this is probably a joy for anyone who's a fan of football games to kind of go back and play and see how it works.

01:05:20.300 --> 01:05:22.340
And see that it works really well and still is functioning.

01:05:22.780 --> 01:05:25.180
I just think that there are just so many other options out there.

01:05:25.220 --> 01:05:34.240
I can't imagine that this would be, that this is something that you should play if it's not something, if you're not like interested in old versions of this game.

01:05:34.300 --> 01:05:42.020
You know what I mean? I mean, like, I don't think, like, a bunch of my friends who are, you know, who play Madden, I don't know if I would tell half of them, like, oh, you've got to play Tecmo Bowl.

01:05:42.300 --> 01:05:48.280
I think that it's more a niche for people who are really into, like, retro versions of the game.

01:05:48.940 --> 01:05:51.700
So, for that being said, I'm going to give it a no.

01:05:52.220 --> 01:05:55.880
But just know that I agonized over that decision.

01:05:56.240 --> 01:05:59.460
It's understood. You almost gave us a shark tank there.

01:05:59.540 --> 01:06:01.700
You almost were like, and for that reason, I'm out.

01:06:01.900 --> 01:06:03.660
You know, like, it was good.

01:06:04.460 --> 01:06:06.100
Sean, your vote.

01:06:06.480 --> 01:06:15.540
I very much respect Joe's logic here because that's basically been my thought process going into every sports game.

01:06:15.720 --> 01:06:17.900
I've been fascinated by almost all of them.

01:06:19.480 --> 01:06:42.700
But even knowing that Tecmo Super Bowl is shaping up to be the better game in the long run here, I think I have to waive the sports game edict of the NES, of this nostalgia essential games list.

01:06:42.700 --> 01:06:55.400
I know that I might be the only vote here, but I at least need to put in the symbolic vote of putting this on the essential games list because I think that they did so many things right.

01:06:55.400 --> 01:06:56.760
The game feels great.

01:06:57.960 --> 01:06:59.700
It feels like football.

01:06:59.700 --> 01:07:04.920
And it feels like football beyond just like, yes, these are the rules of football.

01:07:05.100 --> 01:07:06.840
It's just a hell of a lot of fun.

01:07:07.220 --> 01:07:08.920
All right. That is one vote.

01:07:10.140 --> 01:07:12.160
Sam, please give your vote.

01:07:12.160 --> 01:07:22.420
So this game, I think something that we touched on a little bit, but I didn't explicitly say is it's very nuanced for what you get.

01:07:22.760 --> 01:07:27.860
And these all the players have their own stats, which impacts the game.

01:07:27.860 --> 01:07:39.140
And it lets you over time, I would imagine, pick your favorite team or pick how you want to play based on the players on the team and the four play options that you do get.

01:07:39.660 --> 01:07:44.420
And for me, I don't really play a lot of football games or any football games.

01:07:44.640 --> 01:07:48.120
This is really the first time in a long time that I played a football game.

01:07:48.200 --> 01:07:54.460
I think my friend had an SNES and we played one of the Maddens for that once.

01:07:54.460 --> 01:07:55.520
And it was it was fun.

01:07:55.640 --> 01:07:57.580
But, you know, that's beside the point.

01:07:58.040 --> 01:07:59.840
But this one is really fun.

01:08:00.120 --> 01:08:02.220
It's a great football experience.

01:08:02.720 --> 01:08:09.640
And in my book, it's essential because it's just perfect for what it's trying to accomplish.

01:08:10.060 --> 01:08:12.320
Oh, man, I'm so glad you joined me.

01:08:14.000 --> 01:08:15.200
Well, it's close.

01:08:15.200 --> 01:08:16.340
It's close. That's two.

01:08:17.040 --> 01:08:17.900
You know, you need three.

01:08:18.100 --> 01:08:20.140
I know. And that means that you need me.

01:08:20.900 --> 01:08:22.040
You son of a bitch.

01:08:22.220 --> 01:08:23.700
Because it rhymes. Three and me.

01:08:24.860 --> 01:08:25.760
Twenty. Forty.

01:08:25.840 --> 01:08:27.560
Twenty. Three and me. Yeah.

01:08:27.780 --> 01:08:38.320
You know what's funny? Everything about this, like, Tecmo Bowl experience that I've had, it makes me think of what I want in a sports game, right?

01:08:38.520 --> 01:08:41.600
Like, forget about being on the NES or anything like that.

01:08:41.680 --> 01:08:45.220
Like, it's just it's got to be fun, first and foremost, to play a sports game.

01:08:45.220 --> 01:08:53.440
There are weird moments where I want to play something like out of the park baseball, which you're just like the general manager of a team and nothing exciting happens.

01:08:53.680 --> 01:08:55.580
But that's just because I really love baseball.

01:08:55.780 --> 01:08:58.480
That's not actually like a fun experience for almost anybody.

01:08:59.380 --> 01:09:05.200
This is a fun experience that, like, the sports genre of it makes it feel like a qualifier.

01:09:05.640 --> 01:09:09.900
If we're just putting in sports games, it's in a league of its own right now.

01:09:10.100 --> 01:09:14.920
That's, this is the top of the genre right now for sports games on the NES, hands down.

01:09:14.920 --> 01:09:18.960
But I'd argue that this isn't even a sports game.

01:09:19.140 --> 01:09:24.760
Like, this is, this is the same kind of thing we were talking about when we were talking about Mike Tyson's Punch Out being like a rhythm game.

01:09:24.920 --> 01:09:34.540
This is just a fun arcade-y style game that happens to have most of the rules of football, but also exists as, like, just a fun video game.

01:09:34.640 --> 01:09:39.700
It doesn't exactly emulate and presentationally give you football.

01:09:39.860 --> 01:09:44.060
It just takes the football rules and figures out a way to make them work on an NES with limited restrictions.

01:09:44.660 --> 01:09:49.740
I'd argue that most of the restrictions that they made actually made the game better than ever made it worse.

01:09:50.280 --> 01:09:54.860
And for that reason, this is going on the Essential Games list.

01:09:55.020 --> 01:09:56.160
Oh, hell yeah.

01:09:58.320 --> 01:09:59.400
Joe, how do you feel?

01:09:59.600 --> 01:10:05.400
You know, I suspected that, honestly, I was surprised that Sean was, thought he was going to be the only vote,

01:10:05.460 --> 01:10:07.080
because I suspected that you both were going to put it on.

01:10:07.160 --> 01:10:11.820
I did not expect Sam to put it on, but, you know, I got no issue with it.

01:10:12.100 --> 01:10:13.900
All right. I mean, like, look, this is what happens.

01:10:13.900 --> 01:10:16.180
We finally have an Essential Game in 1989.

01:10:16.380 --> 01:10:23.400
I doubt it will be our only one, but it's also only safe until, according to my math,

01:10:23.720 --> 01:10:28.560
it's only safe until December 2nd, 2022.

01:10:29.180 --> 01:10:30.340
I guess we'll see.

01:10:30.560 --> 01:10:31.400
Oh, that's, oh my God.

01:10:31.500 --> 01:10:33.600
We'll see. That's so far away from now.

01:10:33.660 --> 01:10:36.060
But also... So it's going to be on the Essential Games list for a long time,

01:10:36.080 --> 01:10:39.500
and Zelda 2 will not be on the Essential Games list for a long time.

01:10:40.700 --> 01:10:44.460
So, you know, there are people who are mad about Zelda 2 not being on.

01:10:44.460 --> 01:10:49.120
I just wanted to keep this off to use it as a bargaging chip for Zelda 2 in December of 2022.

01:10:50.160 --> 01:10:51.520
It's an interesting strategy.

01:10:51.520 --> 01:10:53.020
It would have gotten you somewhere, probably.

01:10:53.080 --> 01:10:55.860
Right. I'll say, though, I, you know, if...

01:10:56.300 --> 01:11:01.260
I wouldn't want to be the one to keep this off just because it's not a game for me anyway.

01:11:01.400 --> 01:11:03.820
So I'm glad that if you guys feel this way, I'm glad it got on.

01:11:04.100 --> 01:11:07.240
I'm glad we didn't change the rules so all four of us have to vote.

01:11:08.120 --> 01:11:09.040
Yeah, that's true.

01:11:09.180 --> 01:11:11.380
That's true. That's only for Essential Platinum games.

01:11:12.000 --> 01:11:20.260
Right, right. Anyway, this has been a very long football episode of Nostalgia,

01:11:21.100 --> 01:11:26.660
and next week we promise to deliver probably one of the longest episodes of all time

01:11:26.660 --> 01:11:29.840
when we play Ultima 3 Exodus.

